ends rewriting conditions using the OR operator into a series of UNION ALL operations to enhance code maintainability and performance. Finally, it advises using EXCEPT instead of NOT IN or anti-joins to minimize duplicate row processing and optimize resource use.\n\nThe rewritten query would replace IN with INTERSECT where possible, rewrite OR conditions into UNION ALL operations, and use EXCEPT instead of NOT IN or anti-joins. This could lead to improved performance and maintainability.\n\nQuery Rewrite 3:\nThe original query performs multiple joins on the same table to retrieve different attributes for certain conditions. Rule 3 suggests combining these joins into a single join operation by using CASE statements to conditionally select different attributes from the table in one pass. It also recommends using COALESCE with CASE statements to efficiently merge conditional attributes into distinct columns based on specific criteria without the need for additional joins.\n\nThe rewritten query would combine the multiple joins into a single join, using CASE statements to conditionally select different attributes and COALESCE to merge conditional attributes into distinct columns. This could lead to improved performance, simplified query structure, and better readability and maintenance.\n\nNote that Rule 2 does not apply to this SQL query, so there is no Query Rewrite 2 explanation.", 'time': 18.397261142730713}
